---
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import JsonToPythonTool from '../../components/tools/JsonToPythonTool.astro';
import { toolBySlug } from '../../data/tools';
import { jsonToPython } from '../../lib/python-dict';

const tool = toolBySlug('json-to-python-dict');

// Worked example and every error message below come from the real engine at build time.
const jsonSample = '{"id": 7, "active": true, "ref": null, "tags": ["a", "b"]}';
const pySample = (jsonToPython(jsonSample) as { ok: true; output: string }).output;
const err = (s: string) => {
  const r = jsonToPython(s);
  return r.ok ? '' : r.error.replace(/\.$/, ''); // quoted inline, so drop the message's own full stop
};
const trailingCommaErr = err('{"a": 1,}');
const singleQuoteErr = err("{'a': 1}");
const unquotedKeyErr = err('{a: 1}');
const dupKeys = (jsonToPython('{"a": 1, "a": 2}', { indent: 0 }) as { ok: true; output: string }).output;
const reordered = (jsonToPython('{"2": "b", "1": "a", "x": "c"}', { indent: 0 }) as { ok: true; output: string }).output;
const bigInt = (jsonToPython('{"id": 12345678901234567890}', { indent: 0 }) as { ok: true; output: string }).output;
const singleLine = (jsonToPython('{"a": 1, "b": [1, 2]}', { indent: 0 }) as { ok: true; output: string }).output;

const steps = [
  'Paste JSON (an object, array, string, number, boolean or <code>null</code>) exactly as an API response, config file or <code>JSON.stringify</code> printed it.',
  'Choose the indent: 2 spaces, 4 spaces, Tab, or <em>Single line</em> for one unbroken statement.',
  'Tick <strong>Sort keys alphabetically</strong> if you need a stable order for diffing against another version.',
  'If it does not convert, the error names the character position JSON.parse stopped at. JSON is stricter than Python about quotes, commas and comments.',
  '<strong>Copy to clipboard</strong>, or <strong>Download .py</strong> to save it as a snippet.',
];

const faq = [
  {
    q: 'Why doesn’t Python’s json module just do this?',
    a: '<code>json.loads()</code> already parses JSON into native Python objects; this tool exists for when you don’t have a Python process running, only a browser: pasting an API response or a config file here gives you back the exact literal source you would type to recreate it, ready to paste into a script.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Why are booleans and null capitalized in the output?',
    a: 'That is Python syntax, not a stylistic choice: <code>true</code>/<code>false</code>/<code>null</code> are JSON-only keywords, while Python spells the same values <code>True</code>/<code>False</code>/<code>None</code>. Pasting the JSON spelling into Python source raises a <code>NameError</code>.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does key order change from what I pasted?',
    a: `No. Every key keeps the position it was written in, including digit-only keys such as <code>"2"</code> and <code>"1"</code>, which a plain JavaScript object would move to the front in numeric order. That matches what Python’s own <code>json.loads</code> gives you. Tick <strong>Sort keys alphabetically</strong> if you want a fixed order instead.`,
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I use the result as-is in my code?',
    a: 'Yes. The output is a plain Python literal (dict, list, string, number, boolean or <code>None</code>), so <code>data = &lt;paste&gt;</code> or <code>ast.literal_eval()</code> on it both work without modification.',
  },
];
---

<ToolLayout {tool} {steps} {faq}>
  <JsonToPythonTool />

  <Fragment slot="content">
    <h2>Why JSON needs translating before it is valid Python</h2>
    <p>
      JSON and Python's dict/list syntax look almost interchangeable, which is exactly what makes copying one into the other
      by hand risky: JSON requires double-quoted strings and spells its constants <code>true</code>, <code>false</code> and <code>null</code>, while Python requires the capitalized <code>True</code>, <code>False</code> and <code>None</code>
      and is happy with either quote style. Paste raw JSON into a <code>.py</code> file and it mostly runs, until it hits
      one of those three keywords and raises a <code>NameError</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
      This tool parses the JSON with the same rules a browser's <code>JSON.parse</code> uses, then re-serialises the result
      as Python source (the reverse of the site's <a href="/tools/python-dict-to-json">Python Dict to JSON</a> converter).
      Nothing is <code>eval()</code>'d in either direction.
    </p>

    <h2>Example</h2>
    <p>JSON as an API or config file would show it:</p>
    <pre><code>{jsonSample}</code></pre>
    <p>becomes:</p>
    <pre><code>{pySample}</code></pre>

    <h2>Options explained</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Indent: 4 spaces / 2 spaces / Tab / Single line</strong> sets the output layout. 4 spaces is the PEP 8
        convention this tool defaults to; <em>Single line</em> writes the whole literal on one row (still valid Python,
        e.g. <code>{singleLine}</code>, just not pretty-printed; there is no equivalent of JSON minification for source code).</li>
      <li><strong>Sort keys alphabetically</strong> sorts every object's keys at every depth in code-point order, matching
        Python's own <code>json.dumps(obj, sort_keys=True)</code>; uppercase keys sort before lowercase ones.</li>
      <li><strong>Load sample</strong> inserts a small JSON object with a nested array and a <code>null</code>; <strong>Clear</strong> empties the box.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Edge cases and common errors</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>JSON is stricter than it looks.</strong> A trailing comma (<code>{'{"a": 1,}'}</code>) stops with
        "{trailingCommaErr}"; Python's own dict literals allow trailing commas, JSON does not. Single-quoted JSON, like
        <code>{"{'a': 1}"}</code>, stops with "{singleQuoteErr}", and unquoted keys, such as <code>{'{a: 1}'}</code>, with
        "{unquotedKeyErr}". None of these are valid JSON even though all three are valid Python.</li>
      <li><strong>Comments are not allowed.</strong> Unlike Python's <code>#</code> comments (handled by the site's <a href="/tools/python-dict-to-json">Python Dict to JSON</a> tool), JSON has no comment syntax at all: a <code>//</code> or <code>/* */</code> block anywhere in the input fails the same way a syntax error would.</li>
      <li><strong>Duplicate keys keep the last value</strong>, exactly as Python's own dict literal would if you typed
        it directly: <code>{'{"a": 1, "a": 2}'}</code> becomes <code>{dupKeys}</code>.</li>
      <li><strong>Digit-only keys stay where they were written.</strong> <code>{'{"2": "b", "1": "a", "x": "c"}'}</code> becomes <code>{reordered}</code>. A JavaScript object would list <code>"1"</code> and <code>"2"</code> first in numeric order; this tool reads the keys straight from your text instead, so the order matches what Python's <code>json.loads</code> gives.</li>
      <li><strong>Large integers keep every digit.</strong> <code>{'{"id": 12345678901234567890}'}</code> becomes <code>{bigInt}</code>. Numbers are copied from your text as written rather than through a JavaScript number, which would round anything above 2<sup>53</sup> (this one to <code>12345678901234567000</code>). Python's integers have no size limit, so the literal reads back exactly. <code>1.0</code> stays <code>1.0</code> and <code>1E5</code> stays <code>1E5</code>, both valid Python floats.</li>
      <li><strong>Control characters are escaped</strong>, so the output always parses: a carriage return becomes <code>\r</code> and other invisible characters <code>\x00</code>-style escapes, where a raw one would end the string literal or, for a NUL byte, be rejected as source code.</li>
      <li><strong>Size:</strong> a 4 MB JSON document takes well under a second to parse and re-render on a laptop, and
        typing is debounced so the page stays responsive while you edit.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Do the same thing in code</h2>
    <p>If you're already inside a Python process, you don't need a text conversion at all, since <code>json.loads()</code> gives
      you the native object directly, and printing it already uses Python's own quotes and constants:</p>
    <pre><code>import json
data = json.loads(json_text)
print(data)</code></pre>
    <p class="muted">
      <code>print(data)</code> renders single quotes and <code>True</code>/<code>False</code>/<code>None</code> the same
      way this tool does, but on one line. For a multi-line, indented layout closer to this page's default, use <code>pprint.pformat(data, indent=1, sort_dicts=False)</code>. It will not match this tool's formatting exactly
      (it wraps long lines rather than always breaking one item per line), but it is Python's nearest built-in equivalent.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
